course: dictionary
lesson: 13382
title: Who runs a school?
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A headmaster is the man in charge of a school. The word is made of two parts: head, meaning the leader, and master, meaning a man in charge. «The headmaster rang the bell for assembly.» Here the bell is rung by the person who leads the whole school.
explain: Look at the words around a new word for clues. «Ella was sent to the headmaster's office for talking in class.» Being sent to an office for talking tells you the headmaster deals with serious problems, not small ones in the classroom.
explain: Schools use different words for the same job. In the UK, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand you may hear headmaster or head teacher. In the USA they say principal. Check the story you are reading to see which word the writer chose.
fact: A headmaster is a man in charge of a school.
fact: A woman in charge of a school is a headmistress.
fact: «The headmaster leads the assembly» means he stands at the front and runs the whole school gathering.
fact: Many modern schools say head teacher or just head instead of headmaster.
fact: In the USA, the person in charge of a school is called the principal.
exercise: «The headmaster rang the bell for assembly.» Who rang the bell? | *The headmaster | A pupil | The dinner lady | The cleaner
exercise: What does headmaster mean? | A man who cooks the school dinners | A driver of the school bus | *A man in charge of a school | A teacher who only teaches maths
exercise: What is the word for a woman in charge of a school? | Headlady | Headmother | *Headmistress | Head pupil
test: Modern schools often use another word for headmaster. Which one? | *Head teacher | Pupil | Caretaker | Prefect
test: In the USA, what word is used for the person in charge of a school? | Mayor | Captain | *Principal | Headmaster
exam: Why was Ella sent to the headmaster's office? | *Because she was talking in class | Because it was her birthday | Because she was late for lunch | Because she won a prize
